\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Scope.}
All experiments use Qwen3 (0.6B and 1.7B) with 132--200M tokens of continued pretraining and self-generated contamination.
Generalization to other model families, full pretraining scale ($>$100B tokens), or realistic multi-source web contamination is untested.

\item \textbf{Detector coverage.}
Only three detectors from two families (discriminative TF-IDF+LR and generative perplexity scorers) were evaluated; fine-tuned neural classifiers (e.g., ModernBERT) and zero-shot methods (e.g., DetectGPT, Binoculars) remain untested, so the reported AUC/ECE thresholds may shift with broader coverage.

\item \textbf{Statistical power.}
The primary comparison ($\rho{=}0.4$) uses $N{=}5$ seeds ($p_{\mathrm{BH}}{=}0.011$); robustness analyses ($\rho{=}0.6$, detectors, scale) use $N{=}3$, where $\rho{=}0.6$ falls short of significance under full-family correction ($p_{\mathrm{BH}}{=}0.064$).

\item \textbf{Realistic contamination.}
The self-contamination setup provides known ground-truth labels but represents an easier detection scenario than cross-model contamination, where generator--corpus distribution mismatch may reduce detector accuracy.
\end{itemize}

